p.src:
To put the action into standard mechanical form, factor $dt$ from $d\tau$: $d\tau
=dt\sqrt{1-\dot{\vec x}^{\,2}},
\qquad
\dot{\vec x}^{\,2}
=\dot x^2+\dot y^2+\dot z^2.$ Then $S
=
\int_a^b L\,dt,
\qquad
\boxed{
L=-m\sqrt{1-v^2}
}
\quad(c=1).$ In conventional units, $L=-mc^2\sqrt{1-\frac{v^2}{c^2}}.$ This is the Lagrangian of a free particle: it depends on velocity but not position. No force or potential has yet been introduced.

