p.src:
Because $L$ has no position dependence, it is invariant under spatial translations. The corresponding conserved quantity is momentum. We do not assume the Newtonian formula $p_i=mv_i$; we use the canonical definition $p_i=\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot x^i}.$ For $L=-m\sqrt{1-v^2}$, $\begin{aligned}
p_i
&=
-m\frac{1}{2\sqrt{1-v^2}}
\frac{\partial}{\partial\dot x^i}(1-v^2)\\
&=
-m\frac{1}{2\sqrt{1-v^2}}(-2\dot x^i)\\
&=
\frac{mv_i}{\sqrt{1-v^2}}
=m\gamma v_i.
\end{aligned}$ Since $u^i=\gamma v^i$, $\boxed{
p^i=mu^i
}.$ At low speed this reduces to $p^i\approx mv^i$. Near light speed the denominator matters decisively.

